{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261113T212159Z-5fd8df43", "ts": "2026-11-13T21:25:20Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "feature/testament-evidence-wiring", "build": "1151816623d4"}, "body": {"base_commit": "30d22d8153fd7c38d67a4ce16411ac179932eb99", "base_tree": "df086195fba78b961ce8d9d6fe4039bcbe8ec610", "head_tree": "9069f6270bdac004b1e0d4c0c53a175a14d4a243", "head_commit": "64037f26c4a223869aa7e8da10fc6d90a7378631", "dispatch_commit": "64037f26c4a223869aa7e8da10fc6d90a7378631", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/probe_families.py", "plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "tests/test_testament_wiring_probes.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-testament-evidence-wiring.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wiring.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/probe_families.py", "plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "tests/test_testament_wiring_probes.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-testament-evidence-wiring.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wiring.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "In a mixed test_commands repo the --from-counts refusal tells the caller to ingest only the testament report, which records partial evidence as the whole suite", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the wired branch of `from_counts_refusal` was written for a single `test_command: testament run ...` and drops the generic text's 'repeat once per report'. When testament is one entry among several, which is prawduct's own shape (`.prawduct/project-state.yaml` declares testament, a node junit run and a local pytest run for requires_history), the lead route says to run `testament run --junit-xml <report>` and ingest *that* with `--from-junit <report>`. The recorder trusts the operator's claim that the reports are the declared commands' full output and does not count reports against `declared_commands`, so following the message records green evidence that never ran the node tests or the requires_history tests, and `test-status` then reads it as current. That is the partial-evidence result the recorder's ingest loop says it refuses. Fix: when `len(commands) > 1`, have the testament route name one `--from-junit` per declared command (the testament report plus a report from each other entry), or lead with the bare `record`, which already runs all of them. Rule cited: \"A message naming the caller's NEXT step must ask the gate that will judge that step\". The plan's Verification Strategy calls for 'the refusal's text in each of those states through the real hook', and that list includes 'present and wired ... through one `test_commands` entry'. `TestFromCountsRefusal` covers only the scalar-unwired, scalar-wired and no-manifest states, so this branch has no test. Add a hook-subprocess test on prawduct's own multi-entry shape that asserts the message does not tell the caller to ingest only the one testament report.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "tests/test_testament_wiring_probes.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "blocking", "title": "governed_by 'every fact has one home -> conforms' is untrue: the declared-command list gets a second reader that disagrees with the recorder's", "recommendation": "Class. Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: the disposition says the norm holds because 'one function answers whether a declared command runs testament'. That shares the matcher (`runs_testament`) but not the reader that feeds it, and architecture.md's norm names exactly this kind: \"A fact is the whole predicate, not a token inside it: sharing a constant shares syntax; sharing the constant AND the reader that feeds it is what shares the definition.\" The recorder's declared set comes from the hook's `_read_str_list_yaml_key` / `_read_str_yaml_key`, under the narrowing 'DECLARED and non-empty -> items, anything else present -> refuse'. `testament_wiring_probes.declared_commands` re-reads project-state through `lib.core.read_block_sequence` with a different policy: a declared-empty sequence (`test_commands: []`, or a bare `test_commands:` with no items) comes back as `[]`, not as unreadable. Observable divergence: in a testament repo with `test_commands: []`, the recorder refuses ('declared ... but not in the supported shape'), but the probe fires and says the recorder runs 'none (the local pytest fallback)'. That contradicts the module docstring's own 'could not look when test_commands is declared in a shape the recorder refuses'. Following its fix (adding `test_command: ...`) leaves the recorder still refusing and the advisory still firing, because the empty `test_commands` wins in both readers. `from_counts_refusal` also re-reads the file instead of using the `declared_commands` the hook already computed to gate the refusal. Fix by construction: give the recorder's declared-command set one home that the recorder, the refusal (pass the hook's `declared_commands` in) and the probe all go through, with the recorder's refusal policy included. Pin `test_commands: []` plus `.testament.toml` -> probe returns None. Alternatively, record a decision for the departure and correct the disposition.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The advisory's and the refusal's one-line fix assume the scalar form: in a test_commands repo, following it makes the recorder refuse", "recommendation": "Instance (two strings in one module). Both the advisory's `trigger_summary` and the unwired branch of `from_counts_refusal` say 'Declaring `test_command: testament run --junit-xml {junit_xml}` in .prawduct/project-state.yaml'. That text is fixed, not derived from which key the repo declares. In a repo that declares `test_commands` (the case `test_a_list_with_no_testament_entry_fires_and_names_each` fires on), adding `test_command:` as told makes the recorder refuse with 'declares BOTH test_command and test_commands'. The probe still reads the list, so the advisory keeps firing. `project-state-keys.md` gets this right ('or makes it one `test_commands` entry'), but the advisory and the refusal, which are what reach the consumer, do not. Derive the fix text from the declared key: replace an entry or add one for `test_commands`, and the scalar for `test_command` or nothing declared. Extend `test_following_its_fix_silences_it` to the list shape. Rule cited: \"An advisory's printed recommendation ships to every consumer repo \u2014 test its EFFECT\".", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "tests/test_testament_wiring_probes.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The recorder's declared-command resolution now has a second home, and the two already disagree", "recommendation": "Class, not instance. Why it broke: `testament_wiring_probes.declared_commands` re-derives 'which commands the recorder runs' from disk with `lib.core.read_block_sequence`, while the recorder (`cmd_test_evidence`, prawduct-hook ~5352-5381) resolves the same question with `_read_str_list_yaml_key` + `_yaml_top_level_key_present` + the both-declared refusal. Their rules differ. For an empty `test_commands:` block or `test_commands: []`, core returns DECLARED with no items, so the module reads it as 'nothing declared' and the probe fires naming 'none (the local pytest fallback)'. The recorder reads the same declaration as None-but-present and refuses it outright. A file that declares both keys reads here as the list, but the recorder refuses that too. So the module docstring's claim ('Could not look (None) when test_commands is declared in a shape the recorder refuses') is false for both shapes, and an owner in such a repo is told evidence runs locally when it doesn't run at all. The plan's 'every fact has one home' disposition holds for `runs_testament` but not for the declaration it is applied to. Fix by construction: give the recorder's resolution one owner. One way is a lib function returning (status, commands) with the recorder's semantics (empty sequence and both-declared both unreadable) that the hook and this module both call. At minimum, have the hook pass its already-resolved `declared_commands` to `from_counts_refusal`, so the refusal never re-reads what the recorder just read. Search bound: readers of the `test_command`/`test_commands` keys in plugin/lib and plugin/bin (today only these two sites). Add a fixture for the empty-block shape.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Instruction Clarity (Framework-Specific Checks)", "severity": "warning", "title": "In a multi-command repo where one entry runs testament, the refusal's lead route records partial evidence as the whole suite", "recommendation": "Instance, with a modelling cause. `from_counts_refusal` reduces 'wired' to one boolean, `any(runs_testament(c) ...)`, and in the wired branch it tells the caller to run `testament run --junit-xml <report>` and ingest *that* report with `--from-junit`. It omits the generic text's 'whole suite ... repeat it once per report' clause. The recorder's `--from-junit` loop does not check arity against `declared_commands`. So in exactly the shape the module calls wired on purpose (this repo: testament plus `node --test` plus a local `requires_history` pytest), an agent following the refusal ingests only the testament report, and the recorder stamps the other entries' tests as covered without ever running them. A bare `record` is the only route that works there, and the text puts it second. Fix: when the declaration has entries that don't run testament, have the text say that a report is owed for each declared command, or lead with bare `record`. Pin it with a refusal test on the multi-entry fixture the probe tests already use (`test_one_testament_entry_among_several_is_wired`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The refusal's testament route ingests one report even when several commands are declared", "recommendation": "Instance. The plan works through the multi-entry case for the probe ('One test_commands entry that runs testament is enough', with prawduct's own three entries as the positive control). Nobody checked the refusal's lead route against that same case. from_counts_refusal (plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py) puts the testament route first whenever .testament.toml exists: 'run it again as `testament run --junit-xml <report>` ... and ingest that with `test-evidence record --from-junit <report>`'. It does this for both the wired and the unwired branch, whatever the number of declared commands. The generic text it replaces said 'a JUnit report of the whole suite'. This route drops that qualifier. The recorder doesn't compare the number of --from-junit reports with the declared commands; I found no check against len(declared_commands) on the from_junits path in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook. So in a repo shaped like this one (testament, node and requires_history entries), a caller who follows the lead line records evidence from one of three entries. The node tests and the history-dependent tests are never in the record, but it still reads as the suite passing. That is the exact subset-as-suite false green that the --from-counts refusal exists to prevent. Fix: when more than one command is declared, the testament route should say to pass one --from-junit per declared command, or lead with a bare `test-evidence record` (the wired branch already says that runs everything). Add a refusal test using the three-entry test_commands shape next to the existing single-command fixtures.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "tests/test_testament_wiring_probes.py"], "fid": "R-6"}], "counts": {"blocking": 2, "warning": 4, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Plan acceptance criterion 'no sentence in plugin/ claims --from-counts ingests beside a declared command' holds", "recommendation": "Verified by grepping `from-counts` across plugin/ markdown, lib and bin. building.md is corrected. pr/SKILL.md:55 lists --from-counts only for the no-declared-command case and states it is refused otherwise. The test-evidence docstring in the hook already states the refusal. No edit wanted.", "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "test-status current (tree-valid); verify-coverage skipped (coverage_required false)", "recommendation": "No action. Evidence matches tree 9069f6270bda.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The probe reads the state file twice and checks the manifest twice", "recommendation": "`probe_declared_command_skips_testament` calls `uses_testament` and `declared_commands`, then calls `unwired_commands`, which repeats both. The cause is that `unwired_commands`' `None` folds three conditions together (not in use, wired, unreadable), and the probe has to tell 'unreadable' apart. A single tri-state result (not-in-use / unreadable / wired / unwired(commands)) would drop the second read and the inline re-check in `from_counts_refusal`. Two signals, all in one module, so this is an observation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The recorder's generic refusal text now lives in a testament-specific probes module", "recommendation": "Non-testament repos now get their `--from-counts` refusal text from `testament_wiring_probes.from_counts_refusal`. Anyone editing recorder messages in the hook finds the generic route moved into a module named for one tool. The hook comment and import do point there, so it is findable. If the declaration-resolution owner from the finding above lands, the refusal text could move there with it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The module, feature and registration alias use different names", "recommendation": "The module is `testament_wiring_probes`, its FEATURE is `testament-evidence`, and probe_families imports it as `register_testament_evidence`. Advisory ids follow FEATURE, so a grep from an advisory id to its code does not hit the filename. This is naming only, and no edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_wiring_probes.py", "plugin/lib/probe_families.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Generality (Framework-Specific Checks)", "title": "Generality: testament-specific, consistent with existing precedent", "recommendation": "The probe and the project-state-keys paragraph name one tool (testament). This follows the existing `testament_cache_probes` integration and keys on testament's own manifest, and the building.md change is tool-neutral. This is slight specificity that does no harm to generality. No action.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: change fragment consumer note slightly understates what changed", "recommendation": "Records Pass covered both files_oracle entries: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-testament-evidence-wiring.md and .prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wiring.md. I checked every behavioural claim in both against the code: when the advisory fires, that it names the fix, that one testament entry among several counts as wired, that the recorder refuses the same set, the building.md correction, and that prawduct's own first test_commands entry runs testament. All hold. The consumer note says 'Nothing else changes for any repo', but the --from-counts refusal text was reworded for every repo, including repos without testament (new head line, 'whole suite on this tree' wording). No reader acts wrongly because of this, so it clears no bar and needs no edit.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wiring.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Goal 6: failure paths are fail-soft and visible", "recommendation": "No action. The predicate's readers fail soft: an OSError reads as empty, read_block_sequence and read_str_yaml_key never raise. An unreadable test_commands makes the probe return None, which the advisory store treats as 'could not look'; the recorder already rejects that declaration loudly. The refusal is built from the same fail-soft reads, so it can't crash the exit-2 path. Nothing new grows without bound.", "oid": "O-8"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "testament-evidence-wiring", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "30d22d8153fd7c38d67a4ce16411ac179932eb99", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-testament-evidence-wiring.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wiring.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-testament-evidence-wiring.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-13T21:22:03Z"}}

